===============================================================================
Third-party libraries bundled by suite members
===============================================================================

Each extension's own MIT license covers the shim and the LiveCode Builder /
script binding written for this suite. The native libraries they statically
link (or, for OnionXT, the daemon they talk to) remain under their own
licenses, reproduced with their sources:

  sodiumxt        libsodium                     ISC
                  SHA-3 (RHash, via             MIT
                  trezor-crypto; the same
                  files coinxt vendors)
  torrentxt       libtorrent-rasterbar          BSD-3-Clause
                  Boost                         Boost Software License 1.0
  enetxt          ENet 1.3.18                   MIT
  datachannelxt   libdatachannel                MPL-2.0
                  libjuice (ICE)                MPL-2.0
                  usrsctp (SCTP)                BSD-3-Clause
                  plog (logging)                MIT
                  OpenSSL (DTLS)                system-provided, not bundled
  onionxt         (no bundled code — connects to a Tor daemon you run)
  box2dxt         Box2D v3.1.0                  MIT
                  Kenney game assets            CC0-1.0
                  (the Spritesheets/ art the
                  platformer + demos draw)
  coinxt          trezor-crypto                 MIT
                  SHA-2 (Gifford)               BSD-3-Clause
                  SHA-3/Keccak (RHash)          MIT
                  RIPEMD-160 (Litzenberger)     public domain
                  BLAKE-256 (Aumasson)          CC0-1.0
                  BLAKE2b (Neves)               CC0-1.0 / OpenSSL / Apache-2.0
                                                (coinxt elects CC0-1.0)
                  Groestl (Projet RNRT SAPHIR)  MIT

trezor-crypto is MIT as a project, but the subset coinxt vendors includes
third-party implementations that keep their own terms — the six rows above it.
They are all permissive and none is copyleft, but two carry real distribution
requirements: the SHA-2 files are BSD-3-Clause, whose clause 2 binds a BINARY
redistribution, and coinxt commits built libraries. The list is per-file in
coinxt/THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.md.

The full license text for each is reproduced inside the member that bundles it:
in the member's LICENSE file (sodiumxt), in a THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.md next to
the member's LICENSE (torrentxt, enetxt, datachannelxt, coinxt), or alongside
the vendored sources (coinxt/native/vendor/LICENSE, for the MIT parts).
